> >= lo du'u ma kau klama (ma kau)
> >
> >da zo'u la djan djuno lo du'u da is-extension-of lo du'u ce'u klama
> 
> But John may not know that, maybe he doesn't even know what
> an extension is. All he knows is da, the extension, not that
> da is the extension of something else. What you're doing is
> sumti burying instead of sumti raising!

Yes and no. No, because if he knows who went he perforce knows
what an extension is -- if extensions are an integral part of
interrogatives. Yes, because my sentence says (in effect) that
John knows that he knows who went. It's debatable whether that's
a good thing or not; it seems the desired meaning for most but
not all situations we describe as "knowing who went".

He doesn't know da, though -- at least not by the way I defined
extension, which was (in essence) as the set of goers. I could
rethink it, more along the lines you've been working along,
such that the extension is the set of true answers, but I am
not yet convinced that my version above is wrong.
 
